  ],
  "correct": "E",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find the centres and radii",
      "body": "<p>Read both centres and radii from the circle equations. Use the horizontal and vertical differences between the centres to find their distance.</p>",
      "recap": "The distance between two points comes from Pythagoras.",
      "pitfall": "Take square roots when reading the radii.",
      "pause": "Compare the centre distance with the sum of the two radii.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l13",
          "reminder": "The distance between two points comes from Pythagoras."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Look at the gap between the circles",
      "body": "<p>Draw the line joining the centres. Once you have checked that the circles are separate, mark the two circle points that face each other along that line.</p>",
      "recap": "The centre-to-centre line includes both radii as well as the gap.",
      "pitfall": "Subtract both inside parts of the line, not just one.",
      "pause": "Use your lengths to calculate the gap, keeping the square roots exact.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l03",
          "reminder": "The centre-to-centre line includes both radii as well as the gap."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The centres are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so their distance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>9</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>9</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>. Their radii are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>, whose sum is smaller than the centre distance.</p><p>The shortest gap is attained along the line of centres and equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer E: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</strong></p>",
